Let $\mathbb{T}^1 = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ and consider the closed annulus $A = \mathbb{T}^1 \times [-1,1]$ equipped with Lebesgue measure. Let $f \colon A \to A$ be a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism such that:

(i) $f$ preserves Lebesgue measure;
(ii) $f$ is homotopic to the identity;
(iii) $f$ has only finitely many periodic points;
(iv) the restriction of $f$ to each boundary circle $\mathbb{T}^1 \times \{\pm 1\}$ is a circle diffeomorphism with the same rotation number $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$, and $\alpha$ is Diophantine (i.e., there exist constants $C>0$ and $\tau \geq 0$ such that $|\alpha - p/q| \geq C/q^{2+\tau}$ for every $p/q \in \mathbb{Q}$).

Must $f$ be $C^\infty$-conjugate to the rigid annulus translation
$$R_\alpha(\theta, r) = (\theta + \alpha, r) ?$$

